title: The undo selection
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: reference
summary: What undo and redo offer — the labels of the newest change you made, or of the newest undo, still standing — computed from the log alone, in core, by every client; both are reverts.

Both are reverts, applied onto the current state ([[Reverting onto the current state]]):
nothing moves back over the log, so nothing written since — the watcher's work above all
— is lost or left on a dead branch ([[Why the log stops being a tree]]).

!! Undo

Undo walks the log newest first for the newest operation ''the user wrote'' that still
stands, and offers the labels it was written in, innermost first ([[Undo and redo by label]]).
An operation is ''not'' the one when:

* it is the daemon's — `origin = watcher` exactly, not any set origin ([[Operations and their origin]]); failing an origin, a watcher op type. A `trash` operation ''is'' one: you asked for it;
* it is ''itself'' a revert — an undo or a redo; undoing it would be a redo, and treating it as a candidate would make undo a two-state oscillation;
* it has already been undone — by a revert that still stands, resolved along the whole chain;
* it is an `unknown` operation, which carries nothing to invert;
* it is the log's first operation — the root metarecord written at init, or the oldest operation a prune left (`first` in `GET /log`). Undo stops there; `mf log revert <label>` still reaches it.

Undoing the label chosen is a revert of `{"label_id": L, "only": "changes"}`: the daemon
takes the operations of L and of every label nested in it, and keeps those that are not
reverts, not the watcher's, and still standing ([[Revert endpoints]]). The daemon narrows,
not the client: a label — a script's run — may start long before the window the client
read.

!! Redo

Redo walks the log newest first for the newest ''undo'' that still stands — a revert of an
ordinary change — and offers its labels. On the way it steps over the daemon's
operations, the redos, and the undos already redone: pressing redo again goes on to the
undo before. The newest ordinary change still standing ends the search: ''a new change
clears what could be redone'', as in any editor. What it cleared is still in the log, and
an explicit `mf log revert` of the undo reaches it. Redoing label L is a revert of
`{"label_id": L, "only": "undos"}`: its reverts of an ordinary change still standing.

Undo, change, undo, undo — then redo, redo, redo: the first two redos bring back what the
last two undos took, the third finds the change and answers "Nothing to redo."; the
change undone before it stays undone ([[Why undo and redo do not oscillate]]).

!! Where it lives

The selection is a pure function of `GET /log`, in `core::undo`, shared by `mf log
undo`/`redo` and the GUI's `log:undo`/`log:redo`; the daemon has no undo endpoint — it
offers the revert, the client picks. Both read the log in two bounded passes — 500
operations, then 20 000 — rather than one unbounded one: after a large reconcile the log is
millions of rows, and a repository whose last manual change is older than the second
window is told so rather than scanned whole. Liveness inside the window is exact: a revert
is always newer than what it reverts, so the window holds every revert of what it holds.
The revert itself — including the files it moves — is run by `core::navigation`, the
same for both clients.
